  <data name="en_pano27_description"><![CDATA[<div>Welcome to the ancient city of Kano, home of the famous and colourful DURBAR festival, the city is also known as the centre of Commerce. Kano is the oldest city in W Africa and also the third largest city in Nigeria in terms of population and size. &nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>Kano is one of the most beautiful Cities in Nigeria, the city is adorned mostly with ancient beautiful architecture that makes every step you take within the city a memorable one.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>It has been one of the most important trading towns for the Hausa, who had a big empire in the 18-th and 19-th century and are still one of the most influential people of Nigeria. It’s a bustling and interesting city that has grown at a frenzied pace in recent years.</div>]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano31_description"><![CDATA[This is Dala Hill, it stands about 534 meters high and there are 105 concrete steps leading to the top, Dala Hill presents itself as the best starting point for city tour through Kano. The history of Kano started with the early settlers, Maguzawa tribe who assembled there annually at the foot of Dala Hill to receive a sermon from their Chief priest called Barbushe.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano33_description"><![CDATA[It is believed that the first inhabitants of the city settled at the foot of this Hill. Traces of this historical fact are evident as you meander through the area where many of the buildings still retain their rural appeal.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano34_description"><![CDATA[At this point your climb is over and you are presented with beautiful and magnificent view of this ancient city. Looking back, you will appreciate every step you’ve taken to get to this point.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano37_description"><![CDATA[The top of the hill is made of table flat land about the size of a football pitch, with patch of grass and red stone, this spot offers a spectacular view of this ancient city with its modernity. This is the best starting point when you want to tour the city because, once you go up the hill you can see all part of the city as this will give an idea of places you will be visiting in this ancient city.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano36_description"><![CDATA[Today visitors can climb to the top of the Hill from where you will have a magnificent panoramic view of both old and new Kano. If you wake up early in the morning, make an effort to climb Dala Hill at 6:00 am to watch the sunrise over the Kano city, it’s an unforgettable view.]]></data>






  <data name="en_pano29_description"><![CDATA[<div>This is the Ancient City Wall called Badala by the locals, it dates back to some eight hundred years (800yr) years, . The circular wall (earth structure), was built to protect and secure inhabitants of the city against external invaders. Its total radius is about 25km with height of about e 10meters.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>There are about 18 gates in Kano. They were used to control movements of people in and out of the city in the olden days. Presently the gates serve as entrance and exit to the city through the old city wall or Badala in the local language.</div><div><br></div><div>The Old City (surrounded by the wall) houses the vast Kurmi Market, known for its crafts, while old dye pits - still in use - lie nearby. Also in the Old City are the Emir's Palace, the Great Mosque, and the Gidan Makama Mosque and one of the main tourist attraction, the Dala Hills.</div>]]></data>





